module cdb.parse;

import cdb.lex;

// The SQL expression parser and renderer (docs/m1-lowering.md sections 1, 2 and 7: slice P1b), sans-io: tokens in, a tree in
// caller-supplied arrays out, and a rendering of the tree back to SQL.
//
// THE RENDERING IS WHAT RUNS. The proxy never forwards the client's bytes: it forwards `render` of what was parsed here, so a
// parse that differs from PostgreSQL's is a different statement run, not a bypass. Every operator is therefore parenthesised
// in the rendering, and the gate (tests/parse_diff.py) is that PostgreSQL's own plan for the original and for the rendering
// are the same, and that anything this parser accepts PostgreSQL accepts.
//
// Nothing here recurses and nothing allocates. Expressions are parsed by operator-precedence climbing with two explicit
// stacks (values, and operators with the brackets of calls, lists, CASE and CAST as frames among them), and rendered with a
// third. A deep expression is a refusal (`nested-too-deep`), not a stack overflow.
//
// What is accepted (the rest is refused, never passed through): number, string, NULL, TRUE, FALSE; a column `c` or `t.c`; the
// operators `or and not = <> != < <= > >= + - * / % || like ilike (and NOT of each) between in`, unary minus, `is [not]
// null`; `CASE`; `CAST(x AS type)` and `x::type`, the types of `type_code`; and a call `f(args)` or `count(*)`. PostgreSQL's
// reserved and column-name keywords are refused as names, so a word PostgreSQL would reject is not quietly quoted into one it
// accepts.
//
// The tree: nodes are six ints, `kind data kfirst kcount x y`; node 0 is unused. A node's children are
// `kids[kfirst .. kfirst + kcount]`. Per kind (`data`, `x`, `y`):
//
//     NUM STR            data = the token                    NULL TRUE FALSE: no data
//     COL                data = first name token, x = second name token or -1
//     BIN                data = operator code; two children (for IN, the right one is a LIST)
//     NOT NEG            one child                           ISNULL: data = 1 for IS NOT NULL, one child
//     BETWEEN            data = 1 for NOT BETWEEN; children x, low, high
//     LIST               the items of IN (...)
//     CALL               data = the name token, x = 1 for count(*); the arguments
//     CASE               x = 1 if there is an operand, y = 1 if there is an ELSE; children: operand?, (when, then)*, else?
//     CAST               data = type code, x and y = the modifiers or -1; one child

// Node kinds.
pub fn n_num() -> [] int {
    return 1;
}

pub fn n_str() -> [] int {
    return 2;
}

pub fn n_null() -> [] int {
    return 3;
}

